{#
    The way on from this page: the pages either side of it, at the end of the
    column.

    Nothing of the renderer's gives way here. Its own prev/next block has sat
    commented out of the core template for years, and there is no `next` or
    `prev` to read. So the order comes from `Navigation\Pager`: the tree
    flattened. That is the order the rail lists and the order somebody takes
    through a manual front to back.

    What that order becomes is `sds-nav-pager`'s, not this file's. Which end
    each control sits at, which glyph carries the direction and what a reader
    who cannot see it hears are decisions about a component. A template that
    makes them is a second place to make them differently.

    Handed over as four attributes and not as one object. A label and a target
    each fit in a string, so nothing of this renderer's data model reaches the
    component. A caller who works from some other tree fills the same four.
#}
{%- set steps = pager() -%}
{%- if steps.previous or steps.next %}
<sds-nav-pager
    {%- if steps.previous %} previous-href="{{ steps.previous.href }}" previous-label="{{ steps.previous.label }}"{% endif -%}
    {%- if steps.next %} next-href="{{ steps.next.href }}" next-label="{{ steps.next.label }}"{% endif -%}
></sds-nav-pager>
{%- endif -%}
